      <!-- INTRO -->
      <p><strong>Aerosol self-tanning formulation</strong> is the science of delivering dihydroxyacetone (DHA), a colour-developing active, as a fine, even mist that reacts with surface skin proteins to produce a temporary bronzed appearance without UV exposure. It works by combining a reactive sugar chemistry with an aerosol delivery system engineered for droplet uniformity, propellant compatibility, and colour-guide visibility. For product developers and startup formulators entering the sunless tanning category, understanding how DHA chemistry, spray pattern engineering, and propellant selection interact is essential to producing a stable, even-developing, market-ready aerosol product.</p>

      <!-- SECTION 1 -->
      <section id="section-1">
        <h2>How Aerosol Self-Tanning Formulation Works</h2>
        <p>The active mechanism behind every self-tanning aerosol is the Maillard reaction — a well-documented, non-enzymatic browning reaction between a reducing sugar and free amino groups. In sunless tanning products, dihydroxyacetone reacts with amino acids present in keratin proteins in the stratum corneum, the outermost, non-living layer of the epidermis. This reaction produces brown-to-orange polymeric pigments called melanoidins, which is why a DHA-developed tan is chemically distinct from a UV-induced melanin tan and provides no photoprotective benefit. Because the reaction depends on skin surface chemistry rather than living cellular processes, colour development continues for several hours after application and is strongly influenced by the condition of the stratum corneum at the time of use.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Reaction site:</strong> Outer keratinised skin layer (stratum corneum), not living epidermal cells</li>
          <li><strong>Reaction type:</strong> Maillard (non-enzymatic browning) reaction between DHA and skin amino acids</li>
          <li><strong>Onset:</strong> Visible colour begins within 1–2 hours; full development within 24 hours</li>
          <li><strong>Fade mechanism:</strong> Natural desquamation (skin cell turnover) removes the coloured outer layer over several days</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Because the tan sits entirely within the stratum corneum, pre-application exfoliation is a well-established variable affecting both evenness and depth of colour — thicker, drier skin regions such as elbows, knees, and ankles typically absorb more DHA and develop darker, which formulators and consumers alike must account for. For a broader grounding in how active ingredients interact with delivery systems across the category, see our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/cosmetics-and-personal-care-aerosols-guide" target="_top">Cosmetics &amp; Personal Care Aerosols guide</a>.</p>
      </section>

