      <!-- SECTION 2 -->
      <section id="section-2">
        <h2>DHA vs Erythrulose: Comparing Self-Tan Active Chemistry</h2>
        <p>Dihydroxyacetone is the primary colour-developing active across virtually all sunless tanning products, aerosol or otherwise, and its chemistry and safety profile are well documented. According to <a href="https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PubChem chemical data</a>, DHA (CID 670) is a small three-carbon ketose sugar with high water solubility, which supports its rapid diffusion into the stratum corneum but also makes it reactive and pH-sensitive during formulation and storage. Erythrulose, a four-carbon sugar, undergoes the same Maillard chemistry but at a measurably slower rate, which formulators use to extend colour development and shift the final hue. Combining the two actives is a well-established formulation strategy for a more natural-looking, longer-developing result.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Property</th>
                <th>DHA (Dihydroxyacetone)</th>
                <th>Erythrulose</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Carbon chain length</td><td>3-carbon ketose (triose)</td><td>4-carbon ketose (tetrose)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Reaction rate</td><td>Fast — visible colour within 1–2 hours</td><td>Slower — colour builds over 24–48 hours</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Typical resulting tone</td><td>Warmer, orange-leaning brown</td><td>Cooler, more neutral brown</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Common formulation role</td><td>Primary active, fast colour</td><td>Secondary active, colour longevity and tone correction</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Regulatory status (US)</td><td>FDA-approved colour additive for external cosmetic use</td><td>Permitted cosmetic ingredient; not separately FDA colour-additive listed</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>The primary keyword driving formulation decisions in this category — <strong>aerosol self-tanning formulation</strong> — depends heavily on getting this active ratio right, because DHA-erythrulose blends behave differently in terms of dry time, tackiness, and staining risk on fabric, all of which are amplified or dampened by the aerosol delivery format compared to lotions or gels. Selection of active concentration and ratio remains a formulation-specific decision guided by target tone and desired development speed, not a fixed industry formula.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 3 -->
      <section id="section-3">
        <h2>Aerosol Delivery &amp; Spray Pattern Engineering</h2>
        <p>Converting a DHA solution into a well-performing aerosol product requires spray pattern engineering distinct from other personal care aerosol categories. Unlike a hairspray or deodorant, where a slightly uneven mist has minimal visible consequence, self-tanning aerosols reveal deposition inconsistency directly as visible streaking or patchiness once colour develops. Valve orifice diameter, actuator design, and propellant vapour pressure jointly determine droplet size distribution and spray cone geometry, all of which must be tuned for even coverage at typical application distance.</p>
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Key Insight</strong>
          Because DHA colour development is delayed by one to two hours, spray pattern defects are invisible at the moment of application. This makes actuator and valve validation — rather than visual inspection during use — the primary quality control lever for deposition uniformity in aerosol self-tanning products.
        </div>
        <h3>Droplet Size and Coverage</h3>
        <p>Finer droplet distributions generally produce more even coverage but slower drying and greater risk of overspray drift, while coarser droplets dry faster but risk visible mottling if overlap is inconsistent. Formulators balance this trade-off against the practical constraint that self-tanning aerosols are typically applied at arm's length across large body surface areas, unlike more localised personal care sprays. As covered in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-chemistry-101" target="_top">Aerosol Chemistry 101 guide</a>, carrier solvent choice also affects droplet formation at the valve orifice, which is a further variable specific to self-tanning formulation work.</p>
      </section>

