      <!-- SECTION 4 -->
      <section id="section-4">
        <h2>Propellant Selection for DHA-Stable Aerosol Systems</h2>
        <p>Propellant selection for a DHA aerosol tanning spray must satisfy the same physical performance requirements as any cosmetic aerosol — appropriate vapour pressure, spray characteristics, and can compatibility — while also avoiding chemical interaction with a reactive sugar active. DHA is prone to degradation through the same Maillard pathway that creates the tan; elevated temperature, moisture ingress, and certain pH conditions can trigger premature browning inside the can before the product ever reaches the skin. Propellant and carrier system choice must therefore account for long-term chemical compatibility, not just spray mechanics.</p>
        <div class="callout callout-amber">
          <strong>Rule of Thumb</strong>
          Any propellant or carrier system change in a DHA-based aerosol requires a full accelerated stability study before commercial release. Because DHA's reactivity is the same chemistry responsible for the tanning effect, formulation changes that seem physically minor can measurably shorten shelf life or cause visible in-can discolouration.
        </div>
        <p>Hydrocarbon propellant blends (isobutane/propane systems) remain common in cosmetic aerosols generally, offering fine, consistent mist characteristics well suited to even DHA deposition. Compressed gas systems using nitrogen are also used in some sunless tanning aerosols, particularly where formulators want to minimise propellant contact with the DHA-active phase, since compressed gases in a bag-on-valve configuration physically separate propellant from product. <a href="https://webbook.nist.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NIST WebBook thermodynamic data</a> on hydrocarbon propellant vapour pressure curves informs the can pressure and spray rate targets formulators use when validating a new self-tan aerosol system.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 5 -->
      <section id="section-5">
        <h2>Regulatory Compliance for Sunless Tanning Aerosols</h2>
        <p>Regulatory treatment of self-tanning aerosols differs meaningfully between the United States and the European Union, and product developers targeting both markets must account for both frameworks from the outset of formulation work. In the US, the <a href="https://www.fda.gov/cosmetics" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FDA regulates DHA as an approved cosmetic colour additive</a> for external application, but has specifically noted that it has not evaluated DHA safety for use in a spray or mist format that could result in incidental inhalation or contact with eyes and mucous membranes — a distinction directly relevant to any aerosol self-tanning product, since spray delivery inherently creates airborne exposure that lotion or gel formats do not.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>US:</strong> DHA approved as external cosmetic colour additive (FDA); no separate aerosol-specific DHA clearance, but general cosmetic aerosol labeling and safety requirements apply</li>
          <li><strong>EU:</strong> Regulated under <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A32009R1223" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EU Cosmetics Regulation 1223/2009</a>, requiring a Cosmetic Product Safety Report and CPNP notification before market placement</li>
          <li><strong>Ingredient classification:</strong> DHA and other formulation ingredients must be checked against the <a href="https://echa.europa.eu/regulations/reach" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ECHA REACH</a> registration database for any market where the product will be sold</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Because aerosol delivery inherently produces airborne droplets, formulators should design application instructions — recommended spray distance, avoidance of the face, and use in ventilated areas — that reduce inhalation exposure risk, and should reference current FDA guidance directly rather than assuming lotion-format DHA safety data extends automatically to spray formats. This is a category-specific compliance consideration that distinguishes aerosol self-tanning products from other DHA delivery formats.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 6 -->
      <section id="section-6">
        <h2>Formulation Stability &amp; Shelf-Life Considerations</h2>
        <p>DHA's reactivity is a double-edged sword for formulators: the same Maillard chemistry that creates the desired tanning effect on skin will also slowly darken the product itself in the can if pH, temperature, and moisture are not controlled. Aerosol self-tanning products typically require pH buffering to slow premature DHA degradation, protection from elevated storage temperatures, and packaging materials confirmed compatible with the active system to prevent catalytic degradation from trace metal contact. Accelerated stability testing under standard cosmetic industry temperature-cycling protocols is the primary method for establishing a validated shelf life before commercial launch.</p>
        <h3>Colour Guide and Bronzer Additives</h3>
        <p>Because DHA-driven colour takes hours to become visible, many aerosol self-tanning formulations incorporate a temporary cosmetic bronzer or colour guide — typically a wash-off pigment or water-soluble dye — so users can see exactly where product has landed during application and avoid streaking or missed patches. This guide colour is chemically independent of the DHA reaction and rinses away on first contact with water, while the true DHA-developed tan remains on the skin. Formulators must verify that any guide colourant is compatible with the aerosol delivery system and does not interfere with DHA stability or staining behaviour on skin, hair, and fabric.</p>
        <p>For teams moving from lab-scale DHA formulation trials to a commercial aerosol production run, the transition introduces additional variables — fill line compatibility with a reactive active, batch consistency validation, and packaging material qualification — that are covered in more depth in our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/from-lab-to-commercial-launch" target="_top">lab-to-commercial launch guide</a>. Manufacturers evaluating co-packing or private-label aerosol production specifically for cosmetic actives should also review our <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/cosmetic-aerosol-manufacturing" target="_top">cosmetic aerosol manufacturing overview</a> and, where UV-protective claims are being considered alongside a self-tan line, our dedicated guide to <a href="https://www.globalaerosols.com/aerosol-sunscreen-spray" target="_top">aerosol sunscreen spray formulation</a> for the distinct regulatory and stability requirements that apply to SPF products.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ -->
