        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How long does it take for an aerosol self-tanner to develop colour?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Visible colour typically begins developing within one to two hours of application and continues deepening for six to eight hours as the Maillard reaction proceeds across the stratum corneum. Full colour development is generally complete within 24 hours, after which the tan gradually fades over three to seven days as the outer skin cells naturally exfoliate.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why do aerosol self-tanning sprays use erythrulose alongside DHA?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Erythrulose is a four-carbon sugar that undergoes the same Maillard browning reaction as DHA but at a slower rate, producing a more gradual colour build and a different chromatic tone. Formulators combine DHA with erythrulose to extend the colour development window, soften the initial orange-toned bias of DHA-only formulas, and improve fade uniformity, since the two actives develop and fade on different timelines.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Is the propellant system in a self-tanning aerosol different from other cosmetic aerosols?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">The propellant selection follows the same principles as other cosmetic aerosols but must additionally be chemically compatible with DHA, which is reactive and pH-sensitive. Hydrocarbon propellant blends and compressed gases such as nitrogen are both used in self-tanning aerosols; the choice affects mist fineness, can pressure, and — critically — the rate at which the propellant system may interact with or dilute the DHA solution during storage.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why do some self-tanning aerosols include a colour guide or bronzer?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Because DHA-driven colour development is invisible for the first hour or more, formulators add a temporary cosmetic bronzer (often a wash-off pigment or dye) so users can immediately see where product has been applied and avoid streaking or missed areas. This guide colour washes off during the first shower, while the DHA-developed tan remains, since the two colour mechanisms are chemically independent.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What causes uneven colour or streaking in aerosol self-tanning application?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Uneven colour most commonly results from inconsistent DHA deposition across the skin surface — caused by variable spray distance, overlapping passes, or naturally uneven skin exfoliation (drier areas like elbows, knees, and ankles absorb more DHA and darken faster). Aerosol spray pattern consistency, droplet size distribution, and even can agitation before use all influence deposition uniformity, which is why spray pattern engineering is a key formulation variable.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Are aerosol self-tanning products regulated as cosmetics or drugs?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">In the United States, the FDA regulates DHA as a cosmetic colour additive approved for external application to the skin, though it has cautioned against use on lips, mucous membranes, or areas near the eyes, and has not evaluated DHA for use in spray or mist form that could result in inhalation or eye exposure. In the EU, self-tanning aerosols fall under the EU Cosmetics Regulation 1223/2009 and require a Cosmetic Product Safety Report and CPNP notification prior to market placement.</div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Can aerosol self-tanning sprays be formulated without alcohol?</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">Yes. While some self-tanning aerosols use alcohol-based carrier systems to aid fast drying and reduce tackiness, alcohol-free formulations using aqueous or emulsion-based carriers are widely available and are often preferred for sensitive or dry skin. The carrier system selection affects dry time, DHA stability, and skin feel, and is an independent formulation decision from the DHA/erythrulose active system.</div>
        </details>
